# IDoc Integration Architecture — Developer Guide

## Overview

Both **master-data-ingestion** and **plant-hierarchy-data-ingestion** use a unified **Handler Registry** pattern for integrating SAP IDoc messages. This architecture enables adding new IDoc types or versions without modifying shared infrastructure code (EventHub SDK subscribers, registry, and core processing services).

----

## Architecture Diagram

```
                          ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
                          │     Azure Event Hub (per family)  │
                          └──────────────┬───────────────────┘
                                         │  event batch
                                         ▼
                          ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
                          │    EventHubIdoc*Service           │
                          │  subscribeToEventHub(...)         │
                          │  → processIdoc(message, family)   │
                          └──────────────┬───────────────────┘
                                         │
                                         ▼
                          ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
                          │     MessageProcessingService      │
                          │  parseEventHubBody(rawBody)       │
                          │  → findHandler(parsed, family)    │
                          └──────────────┬───────────────────┘
                                         │
                                         ▼
                          ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
                          │         HandlerRegistry           │
                          │  .findHandler(rawPayload)         │
                          │  Tries handlers in priority order │
                          │  via canHandle() auto-detection   │
                          └──────────────┬───────────────────┘
                                         │  matched handler
                                         ▼
                     ┌───────────────────────────────────────────┐
                     │              IIdocHandler                  │
                     │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐ │
                     │  │ 1. canHandle()  — version detection  │ │
                     │  │ 2. extract()    — parse IDoc segments│ │
                     │  │ 3. map          — to typed payload   │ │
                     │  │ 4. process()    — call domain service│ │
                     │  └──────────────────────────────────────┘ │
                     └───────────────────┬───────────────────────┘
                                         │  typed payload
                                         ▼
                          ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
                          │       Domain Service              │
                          │  .upsert(TypedPayload)            │
                          │  TypeORM entities → Database      │
                          └──────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Flow Walkthrough (Step-by-Step)

| Step | What happens | Key points to consider |
|------|---------------|------------------------|
| 1. Event arrives in Azure Event Hub | SAP IDoc payload is published to a topic/family-specific Event Hub. | Keep topic-per-family boundaries clear; use stable naming and consumer groups per service intent. |
| 2. EventHub SDK subscriber consumes batch | `EventHubIdoc*Service` subscribes, receives events, and forwards each message to `MessageProcessingService.processIdoc(...)` with `expectedFamily`. | Configure checkpointing correctly (interval + storage container); set conservative batch size when ordering/consistency matters; handle graceful shutdown. |
| 3. Envelope/body is parsed | `MessageProcessingService` normalizes Event Hub body format via `parseEventHubBody(...)`. | Validate payload shape early; fail fast on malformed body; keep parse logic centralized to avoid format drift across subscribers. |
| 4. Handler is resolved by family + `canHandle()` | `HandlerRegistry.findHandler(parsed, expectedFamily)` narrows candidates and selects the first matching handler. | Family scoping avoids unnecessary scans; `canHandle()` must be fast and side-effect-free; registration metadata (`family::version`) must stay unique and intentional. |
| 5. Handler maps and validates records | Selected handler chooses config (`selectConfig()`), extracts typed DTOs, applies filters/validation, and prepares clean domain payloads. | Keep variant detection declarative in mapping configs; prefer typed MES DTO contracts; avoid ad-hoc in-handler transformations that duplicate mapping rules. |
| 6. Domain service persists data | Handler calls domain service (`upsert`/link operations) and writes through TypeORM into ISA-95 tables. | Use natural keys/conflict paths for idempotency; preserve transactional integrity when writing related entities; log with enough business identifiers for traceability. |
| 7. Operational concerns run around the flow | Telemetry, warnings, retries, error recovery, and checkpoint updates run alongside processing. | Never swallow unexpected errors silently; distinguish recoverable vs non-recoverable failures; ensure checkpoints advance only after safe processing points. |

