## How to Add a New IDoc (Step-by-Step)

### 1. Create the Module Folder

```
src/modules/new-idoc/
├── dto/
│   └── new-idoc.dto.ts          # Raw IDoc TypeScript interfaces
├── entities/
│   └── new-entity.entity.ts     # TypeORM entity
├── interfaces/
│   └── new-payload.interface.ts  # Typed payload interface
├── new-idoc.module.ts            # NestJS module
├── new-idoc.service.ts           # Domain service (upsert logic)
├── new-mapping.config.ts         # Declarative field mapping
└── new.handler.ts                # IDoc handler
```

### 2. Define the Raw IDoc DTO

Model the SAP IDoc structure as TypeScript interfaces:

```typescript
// dto/new-idoc.dto.ts
export interface NewEdiDc40 {
  MESTYP: string;
  SNDPRN: string;
}

export interface NewIdocSegment {
  FIELD1: string;
  FIELD2: string;
}

export interface NewIdoc {
  EDI_DC40: NewEdiDc40;
  E1SEGMENT: NewIdocSegment;
}

export type NewIdocDto = Record<string, { IDOC: NewIdoc }>;
export const NEW_MESTYP = 'NEWIDOC';
```

### 3. Define the Typed DTO

This is the contract between the handler and the domain service. Use MES business terms, not SAP field names:

```typescript
// dto/new-idoc.dto.ts
export class NewIdocDto {
  /** EDI_DC40.SNDPOR — 3-char ERP source identifier */
  public erpSource?: string;
  /** E1SEGMENT.FIELD1 — business code */
  public code?: string;
  /** E1SEGMENT.FIELD2 — description text */
  public description?: string;
}
```

### 4. Define the Mapping Configuration

Use the declarative `IdocMappingConfig` system to define how the engine navigates IDoc segments using dot-path notation:

```typescript
// new-mapping.config.ts
import { IdocMappingConfig } from '@/common/mapping/service/idoc-mapper-engine';

export const NEW_HEADER_CONFIG: IdocMappingConfig = {
  id: 'NEWIDOC::v1',
  mestyp: 'NEWIDOC',
  segments: [
    {
      path: 'EDI_DC40',
      fields: [
        { source: 'SNDPRN', target: 'erpSource', transforms: ['erpSource'] },
      ],
    },
    {
      path: 'E1SEGMENT.ZMII_E1SEGMENT',   // ← dot-path for nested segments
      fields: [
        { source: 'FIELD1', target: 'code' },
        { source: 'FIELD2', target: 'description' },
      ],
    },
  ],
};
```

**Available transforms:** `trim`, `sapDate`, `sapDateOptional`, `float`, `int`, `erpSource`, `nullable`, `uppercase`, `lowercase`

**Array strategies:** `'first'` (take first), `'firstWithProp'` (first with property), `'all'` (one record per element)

For the full mapping system reference (dot-paths, array strategies, context injection, repeating segments), see [IDOC_MAPPING_SYSTEM.md](./IDOC_MAPPING_SYSTEM.md).

### 5. Create the TypeORM Entity

```typescript
// entities/new-entity.entity.ts
@Entity('new_table')
export class NewEntity extends TimeStamp {
  @PrimaryGeneratedColumn({ name: 'id' })
  public id: number;

  @Column({ length: 4, name: 'site', type: 'char' })
  public site: string;

  @Column({ length: 18, name: 'code', type: 'varchar' })
  public code: string;
  // ...
}
```

### 6. Create the Domain Service

```typescript
// new-idoc.service.ts
@Injectable()
export class NewIdocService {
  constructor(
    @InjectRepository(NewEntity)
    private readonly repo: Repository<NewEntity>,
    private readonly customLogger: AppLogger,
  ) {}

  public async upsert(payload: NewPayload): Promise<void> {
    // Upsert by natural key
    let existing = await this.repo.findOne({
      where: { site: payload.site, code: payload.code },
    });

    if (existing) {
      Object.assign(existing, payload);
      await this.repo.save(existing);
    } else {
      await this.repo.save(this.repo.create(payload));
    }
  }
}
```

### 7. Create the Handler

The handler selects the right config, extracts typed DTOs, validates, and persists. No `toDto()` translation needed — the mapping config targets match DTO field names directly:

```typescript
// new.handler.ts
@idocHandler(IDOC_FAMILY.newIdoc)   // ← add to IDOC_FAMILY in message-handler.interface.ts
@Injectable()
export class NewHandler implements IIdocHandler {
  private static readonly configs: Array<IdocMappingConfig> = [
    NEW_HEADER_CONFIG,
  ];

  private static selectConfig(
    rawPayload: Record<string, unknown>,
  ): IdocMappingConfig | undefined {
    const idoc = IdocHandlerHelpers.getIdocNode(rawPayload);
    return NewHandler.configs.find((c) => !c.matches || c.matches(idoc));
  }

  private static validate(record: NewIdocDto): boolean {
    return !!record.code;
  }

  private readonly metadata: IdocHandlerMetadata = {
    description: 'NEWIDOC handler',
    idocFamily: IDOC_FAMILY.newIdoc,
    version: 'v1',
  };

  public constructor(
    private readonly newService: NewIdocService,
    private readonly customLogger: AppLogger,
  ) {}

  public canHandle(rawPayload: Record<string, unknown>): boolean {
    const idoc = IdocHandlerHelpers.getIdocNode(rawPayload);
    const ediDc40 = IdocHandlerHelpers.getNode(idoc['EDI_DC40']);
    const mestyp = ediDc40?.['MESTYP'];
    return typeof mestyp === 'string' && /newidoc/i.test(mestyp);
  }

  public getMetadata(): IdocHandlerMetadata {
    return this.metadata;
  }

  public async handle(rawPayload: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    const config = NewHandler.selectConfig(rawPayload);
    if (!config) return;

    const records = extractIdocFields<NewIdocDto>(rawPayload, config);
    await Promise.all(
      records.map(async (record) => {
        if (!NewHandler.validate(record)) return;
        await this.newService.upsert(record);
      }),
    );
  }
}
```

### 8. Wire the Module

```typescript
// new-idoc.module.ts
@Module({
  imports: [TypeOrmModule.forFeature([NewEntity]), ConfigModule],
  exports: [NewIdocService, NewHandler],
  providers: [NewIdocService, NewHandler],
})
export class NewIdocModule {}
```

### 9. Add the family key to `IDOC_FAMILY`

Open `src/apps/idoc/idoc-events/message-handler.interface.ts` and add the new key:

```typescript
export const IDOC_FAMILY = {
  loiwcs: 'LOIWCS',
  loirnh: 'LOIRNH',
  newIdoc: 'NEWIDOC',   // ← add
} as const;
```

### 10. Import Module in IdocEventsModule

Import the new module into `IdocEventsModule` so the handler provider is part of the discovery graph:

```typescript
// idoc-events.module.ts
@Module({
  imports: [
    DiscoveryModule,
    ConfigModule,
    // ... existing imports
    NewIdocModule,  // ← add (handler is auto-discovered)
  ],
  providers: [HandlerRegistry, MessageProcessingService],
})
export class IdocEventsModule {}
```

No `useFactory`, no handler references in providers — the registry discovers all `@idocHandler()` classes automatically.

### 11. Add EventHub SDK Subscriber & Environment Variables

Create a topic-specific EventHub subscriber service and register it in `eventhub-idoc-sdk.module.ts`. Follow the existing pattern used by LOIWCS/LOIRNH:

```typescript
// eventhub-idoc-newidoc.service.ts
@Injectable()
export class EventHubIdocNewIdocService implements OnModuleInit, OnModuleDestroy {
  public async onModuleInit(): Promise<void> {
    // initializeEventHub(config) + subscribeToEventHub(...)
    // processor.processMessage => messageProcessingService.processIdoc(message, IDOC_FAMILY.newIdoc)
  }

  public async onModuleDestroy(): Promise<void> {
    // performFinalCheckpoints(...) + unsubscribe + cleanupEventHub(...)
  }
}
```

Then add the new topic config in `src/apps/idoc/config/eventhub-sdk/configuration.ts` and corresponding env vars in `.env.dist` (name, namespace, consumer group, storage account, checkpoint container, checkpoint interval).

